/*

Node.js is an open-source, cross-platform JavaScript runtime environment that allows developers to run JavaScript code outside a web browser.

Traditionally, JavaScript was used only in web browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, etc.) to make web pages interactive. Node.js changed this by enabling JavaScript to run on servers, desktop applications, command-line tools, APIs, and more.

Node.js was created in 2009 by Ryan Dahl.

Why Was Node.js Created?

Before Node.js:

JavaScript ran only in browsers.
Server-side applications were typically written in languages like:
PHP
Java
Python
C#
Ruby

Developers had to use:

JavaScript for the frontend
Another language for the backend

Node.js solved this problem by allowing developers to use JavaScript for both frontend and backend development.

How Node.js Works

Node.js is built on Google's V8 JavaScript Engine, the same engine used in the browser developed by Google.

Traditional Server Model

Most traditional web servers use:

Request 1 -> Thread 1
Request 2 -> Thread 2
Request 3 -> Thread 3

Each request gets its own thread.

Problems:

High memory usage
Limited scalability
Thread management overhead
Node.js Model

Node.js uses:

Single-threaded architecture
Event Loop
Non-blocking I/O
Asynchronous programming
Client Requests
       |
       V
   Event Loop
       |
       V
Background Operations

Instead of waiting for operations like:

Database queries
File reading
API calls

Node.js continues processing other requests.

This makes Node.js very fast and efficient.

Example
Synchronous (Blocking)
const fs = require('fs');

const data = fs.readFileSync('file.txt');
console.log(data.toString());

console.log("Done");

Execution:

Read File
↓
Wait
↓
Print File
↓
Done

The program waits until the file is completely read.

Asynchronous (Non-Blocking)
const fs = require('fs');

fs.readFile('file.txt', (err, data) => {
    console.log(data.toString());
});

console.log("Done");

Output:

Done
File Content

Node.js doesn't wait for the file operation to finish.

Key Features of Node.js
1. Single Threaded

Node.js uses a single main thread for handling requests.

Benefits:

Less memory usage
Fast execution
Efficient resource management
2. Event-Driven Architecture

Everything revolves around events.

Example:

button.on('click', function() {
    console.log("Clicked");
});

Node.js applications listen for events and react when they occur.

3. Non-Blocking I/O

Operations like:

File handling
Database queries
Network requests

run asynchronously.

This allows Node.js to serve thousands of users simultaneously.

4. Cross Platform

Node.js runs on:

Windows
Linux
macOS

The same code can work across platforms.

5. Fast Execution

Node.js uses the V8 Engine, which compiles JavaScript into machine code.

Benefits:

Faster execution
Better performance
